/// eo9:rt — runtime diagnostics shared by every Eo9 guest.
///
/// This package is part of the runtime contract between the guest SDK and the executor,
/// not an OS capability: it grants no authority, has no root-handle resource, no
/// configuration, and no provider stubs. The capability APIs live in the other `eo9:*`
/// packages.
package eo9:rt@0.1.0;

/// A write-once diagnostics sink for the trap path.
///
/// The guest SDK's panic handler calls `report-panic` with the panic message and source
/// location immediately before it lowers the panic to a wasm `unreachable` trap. The
/// executor stores at most one bounded message per task and surfaces it in exactly one
/// place: the `abnormal(trapped(reason))` outcome of a task that subsequently traps. It
/// is never readable by any guest and is discarded if the task does not trap, so it is
/// not usable as an output channel — which is why every program may import it without
/// it counting against an `only` allow-list (see SPEC "Capability subsets…": restriction
/// governs authority, and this interface carries none).
interface diagnostics {
    /// Record the calling task's panic message ("<message> at <file>:<line>").
    ///
    /// Write-once: the first call wins, later calls are ignored. The executor truncates
    /// over-long messages. Calling this and then not trapping has no observable effect.
    report-panic: func(message: string);
}

/// The configuration entrypoint of a configured component.
///
/// `configure(provider, args)` (SPEC "The capability algebra") re-exports the provider's
/// API interfaces unchanged — direct aliases, the algebra never interposes on the call
/// path — and adds exactly one extra export: this interface, whose parameterless `bind`
/// invokes the provider's own `configure` with the constants baked into the artifact at
/// compose time. `$` and `&` propagate the export through every composition (merging the
/// two operands' entrypoints into one when both carry it, provider's first), so the
/// outermost component of any composition reaches every nested configured provider.
///
/// The executor contract gains one uniform step: after instantiating a component and
/// before the first entry into it, call `bind` if the component exports this interface.
/// Like `diagnostics`, this is runtime contract, not a capability: it grants nothing
/// (the configuration is already part of the artifact's identity), guests never import
/// it, and `only` allow-lists never need to name it.
interface configured {
    /// Apply the configuration baked into this component.
    ///
    /// Idempotent: the first call wins, later calls are no-ops (and report success). A
    /// configuration the provider rejects (an invalid baked value) is returned as the
    /// provider's own error text, so a misconfigured composition fails as a typed
    /// pre-run refusal -- never a trap -- before any of its observable behavior runs:
    /// the executor renders the error ("compose-time configuration refused: <reason>")
    /// and does not enter the program.
    bind: func() -> result<_, string>;
}
